    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. what about the sound though? i noticed when they start spooling up they make that high pitched whistling or whining noise. is that just the air moving through the blades or is there something else causing that specific sound?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The quick pitch whistling sound you hear when a diesel turbocharger starts spooling up is called a \"turbo whine.\" This noise is made primarily by the rapid acceleration of air through the turbine blades and compressor wheel, creating pressure waves that produce a whistling sound. In addition, the faster spinning of the turbine may generate mechanical vibrations that also contribute to this noise. The change in volume and pitch can sometimes tell you about the turbine's speed—typically, as the engine and turbo spire up, the noise becomes louder and starts to rise in pitch. It's essentially the sound of the turbo getting its share of exhaust energy to accelerate the incoming air.",
